    "q-280718": {
      "dom_id": "step-q280718",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $b=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $c=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|+k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0\\cdot 5-4\\cdot (-1))-2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 2)+k((-3)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4-2(-15-8)+3k \\\\  & =50+3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 50+3k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{50}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{50}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
